Requirements, shortlist and vendor negotiation for a twelve-branch retailer that replaced a system it had outgrown.

The retailer had run its stock, purchasing and invoicing on a system that was almost twenty years old. Each branch kept its own spreadsheets to fill the gaps, and head office could not see the same numbers twice in a row.

We started with a three-week requirements study. We talked to store managers, buyers and the finance team, and we wrote down the twenty processes that the new system had to support. Five of them were not negotiable. The rest were useful, but a new system could do without them.

![Software assessment sheets, a laptop and an inventory scanner on a desk](media/vendor-review.jpg)

From a long list of nine vendors, we built a shortlist of three. Each vendor saw the same demonstration script, run with the retailer's own sample data. We scored the answers together with the retailer's team, so that no single opinion decided the outcome.

The negotiation was the hardest part. Licence prices were quoted per branch, per user and per module. We brought the offers to one table and compared the total cost over five years. The chosen vendor accepted a lower licence cost, a phased rollout and a clear exit clause.

Today the branches work from one stock view, and purchasing has a single weekly report. The retailer is now preparing the second phase, which adds the online shop.
